  "answer": "Arteries and veins are two types of blood vessels that have different structures and functions. Arteries carry blood from the heart to different parts of the body, while veins carry blood from the body back to the heart. Arteries have thick, elastic walls to handle the high pressure of blood coming from the heart. They do not have valves. Veins, on the other hand, have thinner walls and contain valves to prevent the backflow of blood. The lumen (the inner space) of veins is larger compared to arteries, which helps them carry a larger volume of blood. The blood in arteries is usually oxygenated, while the blood in veins is de-oxygenated.",
